1
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:14,511
Why, you hardly ate anything
of what I left you last night.

2
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:17,069
You're gonna have to
eat all of this now, Jonathan.

3
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:20,949
All of it. Because I'm not
made out of tuna, you know.

4
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:24,556
Boy, there you are,
living in the lap of luxury.

5
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:27,792
I'm gonna be out there
doing some high-class pandering.

6
00:02:27,960 --> 00:02:29,757
For a worthy cause,
for a worthy cause.

7
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:32,878
Here you go.
Eat them up, eat them up.

8
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:42,637
Mrs. Munson has been here 1 7 years,
and Mrs. Disher's been here 1 2 years,

9
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:45,598
and Mr. Rigilinni's been here
1 5 years.

10
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:48,479
- And now you're being evicted.
- And now we're being evicted.

11
00:02:48,680 --> 00:02:49,954
Sixty days' notice and goodbye.

12
00:02:50,120 --> 00:02:52,634
What kind of a world is it
where something like this can happen?

13
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:55,439
And at Christmas too.
Sounds like something out of Dickens.

14
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:58,114
Well, it's not Dickens.
It's Rollins, the owner.

15
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:00,350
And here comes Mr. Rollins now.

16
00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:01,509
Sir?

17
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:04,433
Sir, you served legal notice
on these tenants to evict them

18
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:07,194
from the building where they live
in order to demolish it

19
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:10,318
so your company can construct
a parking garage on this site.

20
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:11,549
Is that a question or what?

21
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:14,109
I'd like to point out
Mr. Rollins has come here today

22
00:03:14,280 --> 00:03:16,589
to offer a very generous
cash settlement.

23
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:19,877
What good are a couple of dollars
when you don't have a place to sleep?

24
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:21,792
Where you go, madam,
is not my problem.

25
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:24,394
What Mr. Rollins means
is that the tax dollars

26
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:26,551
generated by the structure
to be built on this site

27
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:30,269
will help provide the type of housing
these people so desperately need.

28
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:32,158
We're gonna be dead by then!

29
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:34,709
And so the controversy continues
as the demonstration--

30
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:37,030
- Damn it, stop shoving me.
- Then get out of my way.

31
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:38,758
Well, get out of my way, then.

32
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:43,395
Now, you see
what kind of a man this is?

33
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:45,915
Look, I didn't mean to push him,
for heaven's sake.

34
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:47,798
Let's go, Mr. Rollins.

35
00:03:47,960 --> 00:03:50,474
That potential for violence
we remarked on earlier, Tom,

36
00:03:50,640 --> 00:03:53,279
- has just been transformed into...
- Mr. McCabe, are you alright?

37
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:55,874
I'm fine. Fine.

38
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:58,713
--and the man who's trying
to evict these tenants.

39
00:04:15,960 --> 00:04:18,633
Hey. Merry Christmas, Santa.

40
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:46,837
Yeah, well, it wasn't exactly a public
relations triumph, I can tell you that.

41
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:48,319
The headlines read:

42
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:51,199
''Scrooge Hits Santa Claus.''

43
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:53,792
Yeah, well,
first thing tomorrow morning,

44
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:57,111
I'm gonna start looking
for a replacement for Carl Lang.

45
00:04:57,720 --> 00:05:00,712
So I'm gonna have to wait
till after the first of the year.

46
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:04,873
Least by then, all this Christmas stuff
will be out of the way, thank God.

47
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:06,996
When are you coming back?

48
00:05:08,120 --> 00:05:09,997
We have an appointment
with the Rasmussen people

49
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:12,355
tomorrow morning at 9:30.

50
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:15,839
Okay, I'll see you then.

51
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:30,151
What the hell?

52
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:32,151
What are you doing here?
How'd you get in here?

53
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:35,198
Hey, what--?
Get out of here. What--?

54
00:06:46,200 --> 00:06:47,758
Morning, Eleanor. Coffee ready yet?

55
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:49,876
Mr. Harris,
I thought you were still in Savannah.

56
00:06:50,040 --> 00:06:52,395
Oh, I drove up early to beat the rush.

57
00:06:52,560 --> 00:06:55,677
- Is the old man in yet?
- You beat him too.

58
00:06:56,840 --> 00:06:57,909
You're back.

59
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:00,469
Oh, hey,
I saw that Santa Claus fight on TV.

60
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:02,790
Shrewd PR move, Carl.

61
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:18,912
Eleanor, what is it?

62
00:07:23,760 --> 00:07:24,954
Don't touch anything.

63
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:27,156
Call the police.

64
00:07:32,480 --> 00:07:34,914
You're gonna have to stop
being so fussy about what you eat

65
00:07:35,080 --> 00:07:37,310
or we'll have to find you
a rich owner.

66
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:39,232
Yeah.

67
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:42,956
Walk right in. Everybody else does.

68
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:47,033
- You Tom McCabe?
- That's right.

69
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:49,953
We're police officers, Mr. McCabe.
We'd like to know

70
00:07:50,120 --> 00:07:52,509
where you were between the hours
of 9 and midnight last night.

71
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:55,513
I was right here being stabbed
by some burglar.

72
00:07:56,240 --> 00:07:58,708
I was gonna call you guys up,
but I figured, why bother?

73
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:00,472
Emergency hospital report it?

74
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:02,631
They confirmed
that you were cut on the chest.

75
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:04,119
Where were you last night, Tom?

76
00:08:04,280 --> 00:08:05,713
Right here.

77
00:08:05,880 --> 00:08:07,359
Anybody able to corroborate that?

78
00:08:07,520 --> 00:08:09,431
Yeah. The guy that stabbed me.
Ask him.

79
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:10,715
Can you prove you were here?

80
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:12,996
What do you mean, can I prove it?
What is this?

81
00:08:13,160 --> 00:08:15,833
We have reason to believe you may
have been elsewhere at the time.

82
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:17,149
What?

83
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:19,072
Do you know a Mr. Alan Rollins?

84
00:08:19,240 --> 00:08:20,309
No.

85
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:22,516
He's the owner of this building.

86
00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:24,678
Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah.

87
00:08:24,840 --> 00:08:26,910
He's the guy that knocked me down,
right outside.

88
00:08:27,080 --> 00:08:29,594
- It was on TV.
- Wanted to get even, did you, Tom?

89
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:32,194
I don't know
what you're talking about.

90
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:34,191
Somebody killed him last night.

91
00:08:34,360 --> 00:08:36,920
He was bludgeoned to death
by a paperweight.

92
00:08:37,080 --> 00:08:40,117
You got cut by Rollins' letter opener
in the process.

93
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:42,119
It wasn't me.

94
00:08:42,280 --> 00:08:45,113
Your fingerprints
were on the letter opener, Tom.

95
00:08:45,680 --> 00:08:47,432
- Bob?
- Yeah?

96
00:08:47,600 --> 00:08:49,556
This was in the trash.

97
00:08:51,680 --> 00:08:52,715
What is that?

98
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:56,077
Some papers
with Rollins' letterhead on it.

99
00:08:56,240 --> 00:08:57,878
Appears to be
an eviction proceeding.

100
00:08:58,040 --> 00:08:59,678
Even has his signature on it.

101
00:09:00,720 --> 00:09:03,678
I never saw that before.
What is this, some kind of frame?

102
00:09:03,840 --> 00:09:06,593
You go get your coat
and I'll read you your rights, Tom.

103
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,468
- Merry Christmas, Ben.
- Oh, Merry Christmas.

104
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:18,710
I'm gonna miss you, in a way.

105
00:09:18,880 --> 00:09:20,074
You going somewhere?

106
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:22,310
Well, the offices are closed
for the holidays, right?

107
00:09:22,480 --> 00:09:24,869
Yeah, but that sounds like goodbye.
You're coming by the house

108
00:09:25,040 --> 00:09:27,759
- for Christmas dinner, aren't you?
- Oh, Ben, I'd really love to,

109
00:09:27,920 --> 00:09:29,592
but me and Cecily
are headed for Vermont.

110
00:09:29,760 --> 00:09:30,749
Vermont?

111
00:09:30,920 --> 00:09:32,911
It's a veritable
winter wonderland, Ben.

112
00:09:33,080 --> 00:09:34,115
Snow, Ben.

113
00:09:34,280 --> 00:09:39,274
Snow sleds and snowmen
and snow frolicking,

114
00:09:39,440 --> 00:09:42,159
and then inside for the warmth.

115
00:09:42,600 --> 00:09:46,957
Warm hot-rum toddies,
warm fireplace,

116
00:09:47,120 --> 00:09:49,350
warm Cecily, warming me--

117
00:09:49,520 --> 00:09:52,512
Ben, you're not getting my drift.
How old are you?

118
00:09:52,680 --> 00:09:53,669
I get it.

119
00:09:53,840 --> 00:09:55,592
- Merry Christmas.
- Merry Christmas, Ben.

120
00:09:55,760 --> 00:09:57,239
Merry Christmas.

121
00:09:57,400 --> 00:09:59,755
- Tyler, this is for you.
- Oh, thank you.

122
00:09:59,920 --> 00:10:01,239
Have a good trip.

123
00:10:01,400 --> 00:10:03,311
- You too.
- Okay.

124
00:10:03,480 --> 00:10:07,678
- ''You too'' what?
- Oh, I wanted to tell you.

125
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:10,991
I've just been invited
to spend Christmas in Philadelphia.

126
00:10:11,160 --> 00:10:12,354
Invited?

127
00:10:13,160 --> 00:10:14,832
Yeah, my friends. These are for you.

128
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:16,911
And you can't open them
until Christmas morning.

129
00:10:17,080 --> 00:10:20,072
- Well, what about your present?
- Oh, I'll get it when I come back.

130
00:10:20,240 --> 00:10:21,753
And I'll spend New Year's Day
with you.

131
00:10:21,920 --> 00:10:23,672
I'll even watch the Rose Bowl
with you.

132
00:10:24,120 --> 00:10:26,031
- Okay.
- Daddy.

133
00:10:26,200 --> 00:10:27,918
And I'll call you Christmas morning.

134
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:31,790
Okay. Okay, have a good trip.
And don't worry about me.

135
00:10:31,960 --> 00:10:33,359
- Alright, Daddy. Thanks.
- Bye.

136
00:10:33,520 --> 00:10:34,999
Bye.

137
00:10:36,440 --> 00:10:39,989
- Oh, Sarah. Merry Christmas.
- Merry Christmas to you, Charlene.

138
00:10:40,160 --> 00:10:42,276
- Have fun.
- Thanks.

139
00:10:42,520 --> 00:10:44,192
- Ben--
- Are you going to Hong Kong?

140
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:46,635
No, I'm not going anywhere.

141
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:49,872
- There is a lady out here to see you.
- Merry Christmas.

142
00:10:50,040 --> 00:10:51,553
My name is Mrs. Spellman.

143
00:10:51,720 --> 00:10:54,109
I represent the tenants committee
for our building,

144
00:10:54,280 --> 00:10:56,316
and we'd like you
to represent Mr. McCabe.

145
00:10:56,840 --> 00:10:58,558
- Who?
- Mr. McCabe.

146
00:10:58,720 --> 00:11:00,233
He's a gentleman in our building.

147
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:02,834
Well, this time of year,
he plays Santa Claus.

148
00:11:04,480 --> 00:11:07,677
He was arrested
for murdering our landlord.

149
00:11:08,880 --> 00:11:10,313
Oh, yeah.

150
00:11:10,920 --> 00:11:12,751
Well, we'd like you to represent him.

151
00:11:13,560 --> 00:11:14,549
Sarah?

152
00:11:14,720 --> 00:11:16,312
Now, we know that you're expensive,

153
00:11:16,480 --> 00:11:18,948
so we're prepared to pay a percentage
of our pension cheques

154
00:11:19,120 --> 00:11:22,157
and social security until such time
as the bill is paid in full,

155
00:11:22,320 --> 00:11:23,719
providing it's reasonable.

156
00:11:23,880 --> 00:11:24,869
Sarah?

157
00:11:25,040 --> 00:11:26,917
Now, there is one thing
you should know:

158
00:11:27,080 --> 00:11:29,719
None of us are that crazy about him.
He's not very pleasant.

159
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:31,711
In fact,
sometimes he's really very unpleasant.

160
00:11:31,880 --> 00:11:33,074
Sarah.

161
00:11:33,240 --> 00:11:36,038
But there was this one time
when these young hoodlums,

162
00:11:36,200 --> 00:11:38,031
they were trying to rob
some of the old people

163
00:11:38,200 --> 00:11:40,316
and, well,
he chased them right away.

164
00:11:40,480 --> 00:11:42,550
Inside, he's a good man.

165
00:11:42,720 --> 00:11:45,678
But I think the police feel they have
a pretty strong case against him.

166
00:11:45,840 --> 00:11:47,319
But he didn't do it.

167
00:11:48,840 --> 00:11:50,068
Now, there is one condition.

168
00:11:50,240 --> 00:11:52,310
He must never know
that we hired you.

169
00:11:52,480 --> 00:11:54,710
He wouldn't accept it,
so don't tell him.

170
00:11:54,880 --> 00:11:58,077
Now, his full name
is Thomas Bartlett McCabe.

171
00:11:58,600 --> 00:12:00,477
And I guess that about covers it.

172
00:12:00,640 --> 00:12:02,039
So we're in business.

173
00:12:04,040 --> 00:12:05,598
- Well--
- On behalf of the tenants

174
00:12:05,760 --> 00:12:09,878
in our building, I wanna thank you
for taking Mr. McCabe's case.

175
00:12:10,640 --> 00:12:14,519
Well, once again, Merry Christmas.

176
00:12:17,760 --> 00:12:20,672
- That's a nice dress.
- Thank you.

177
00:12:21,760 --> 00:12:23,273
Did you call me a while ago?

178
00:12:24,960 --> 00:12:26,109
Kind of.

179
00:12:26,280 --> 00:12:28,510
Oh, I was down the hall. I'm sorry.

180
00:12:28,680 --> 00:12:30,671
What did you want?

181
00:12:32,120 --> 00:12:33,109
I forget.

182
00:12:34,280 --> 00:12:35,713
Where are you going?

183
00:12:35,880 --> 00:12:38,269
To see Santa Claus.

184
00:12:51,720 --> 00:12:53,551
Mr. McCabe?

185
00:12:54,760 --> 00:12:56,955
Still got on your uniform, I see.

186
00:12:58,440 --> 00:13:00,590
Only warm clothes I've got.

187
00:13:00,760 --> 00:13:03,354
- You the lawyer?
- That's right.

188
00:13:05,360 --> 00:13:07,032
Are we gonna cop a plea?

189
00:13:07,200 --> 00:13:08,599
Is that what you want?

190
00:13:09,120 --> 00:13:10,633
Why not?

191
00:13:14,240 --> 00:13:16,470
Well,
you told the police you're innocent.

192
00:13:16,640 --> 00:13:18,995
I am. Since when
does that make any difference?

193
00:13:19,160 --> 00:13:22,835
Well, it might make a big difference
if a person's innocent.

194
00:13:26,400 --> 00:13:28,118
What do you care?

195
00:13:29,600 --> 00:13:31,033
It's my job.

196
00:13:34,720 --> 00:13:37,359
Well, let me see
if I can get you out of here.

197
00:13:37,520 --> 00:13:39,670
This time of year,
this is no place for Santa Claus.

198
00:13:53,920 --> 00:13:56,753
Your Honour, I respectfully request
the court to take into consideration

199
00:13:56,920 --> 00:13:58,797
that the defendant
has neither steady employment

200
00:13:58,960 --> 00:14:02,111
nor roots in the community,
and to deny bail.

201
00:14:04,840 --> 00:14:09,072
Your Honour, I don't think it's quite fair
for the prosecution to say

202
00:14:09,240 --> 00:14:11,993
that my client
has no steady employment.

203
00:14:12,160 --> 00:14:15,550
It's not his fault that being Santa Claus
is seasonal work.

204
00:14:15,720 --> 00:14:17,312
Your Honour, it's bad enough

205
00:14:17,480 --> 00:14:19,710
that Mr. Matlock dressed his client up
in this costume.

206
00:14:19,880 --> 00:14:23,270
The state strongly objects to his trying
to milk the sentiments of the season.

207
00:14:23,440 --> 00:14:26,796
Well, the court will try
to keep its sentiments in check.

208
00:14:26,960 --> 00:14:30,350
Nevertheless, Mr. Matlock,
this is a legal proceeding.

209
00:14:30,520 --> 00:14:34,274
Of course, Your Honour,
and I will make no further reference

210
00:14:34,440 --> 00:14:37,318
to the fact that my client
symbolically represents

211
00:14:37,480 --> 00:14:42,076
the spirit of loving generosity the world
so sorely needs at this time of year.

212
00:14:42,240 --> 00:14:44,879
Certainly, though,
the court could see its way clear

213
00:14:45,040 --> 00:14:47,600
to take judicial notice
of this spirit and tradition

214
00:14:47,760 --> 00:14:50,877
as it determines the appropriate bail.

215
00:14:51,200 --> 00:14:53,509
Besides, he needs time
to get his sleigh ready.

216
00:14:57,200 --> 00:15:00,158
Bail is set at $1 0,000.

217
00:15:00,320 --> 00:15:03,392
Now, can we have
a preliminary hearing on the 21 st?

218
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,468
No, I'm sorry, Your Honour.
That's not good for me.

219
00:15:07,640 --> 00:15:10,200
What about the 23rd?

220
00:15:10,360 --> 00:15:11,759
Prosecution will be ready.

221
00:15:12,320 --> 00:15:13,389
Mr. Matlock?

222
00:15:14,240 --> 00:15:16,435
The day before Christmas Eve
seems appropriate.

223
00:15:16,600 --> 00:15:20,115
All right. The 23rd, 9 a.m.,
this department.

224
00:15:20,840 --> 00:15:24,958
And defendant is cautioned
not to leave the city of Atlanta.

225
00:15:25,440 --> 00:15:28,477
Not even to go to the North Pole,
Mr. Matlock.

226
00:15:28,640 --> 00:15:30,710
Of course, Your Honour.

227
00:15:32,200 --> 00:15:33,633
I suppose I should thank you.

228
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:36,479
Probably.

229
00:15:39,240 --> 00:15:42,357
May we have the next case
on the calendar, please?

230
00:15:43,720 --> 00:15:46,553
<i>Well, we're still a few days behind</i>
<i>because of that faulty scaffolding,</i>

231
00:15:46,720 --> 00:15:47,709
<i>but we're catching up.</i>

232
00:15:47,880 --> 00:15:49,836
<i>What about you?</i>
<i>How'd the eviction go?</i>

233
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:53,390
<i>Yeah, well, it wasn't exactly a public</i>
<i>relations triumph, I can tell you that.</i>

234
00:15:53,560 --> 00:15:56,757
<i>Triumph? Sounds to me</i>
<i>like a complete disaster.</i>

235
00:15:56,920 --> 00:15:58,148
<i>The headlines read/</i>

236
00:15:58,320 --> 00:16:01,118
<i>''Scrooge Hits Santa Claus. ''</i>

237
00:16:02,120 --> 00:16:03,599
Did Rollins tape all his calls?

238
00:16:03,760 --> 00:16:06,228
Been getting threats
because of that eviction.

239
00:16:06,680 --> 00:16:08,272
What have you got on my client?

240
00:16:09,320 --> 00:16:10,435
Motive.

241
00:16:10,600 --> 00:16:13,034
His fingerprints on the letter opener
found on the floor.

242
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:15,555
No alibi.
Guard downstairs saw him enter.

243
00:16:15,720 --> 00:16:17,233
Nothing much.

244
00:16:17,920 --> 00:16:19,956
The guard downstairs
said he saw somebody

245
00:16:20,120 --> 00:16:22,031
dressed as Santa Claus enter.

246
00:16:22,200 --> 00:16:25,237
He was limping, like your guy.

247
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:30,353
The police report said
the lights were out when you got here.

248
00:16:30,520 --> 00:16:32,317
- Is that right?
- Yes.

249
00:16:33,880 --> 00:16:36,348
Well, did Rollins work in the dark?

250
00:16:36,520 --> 00:16:38,875
Or did the murderer turn the lights out
before he left?

251
00:16:39,040 --> 00:16:42,953
Well, we dusted the light plate.
No prints, if that's what you're thinking.

252
00:16:43,120 --> 00:16:46,271
No, no. I just wonder
why he bothered to turn them out.

253
00:16:46,840 --> 00:16:49,274
Well, maybe he wanted
to conserve energy.

254
00:16:49,440 --> 00:16:53,149
Or maybe he did it so the body
wouldn't be found till the next morning.

255
00:16:53,320 --> 00:16:55,311
I thought we'd find you here.

256
00:16:55,480 --> 00:16:58,358
- Hi. Excuse me a minute, Bob.
- Sure.

257
00:17:00,640 --> 00:17:04,838
I thought the two of you
were going on vacation.

258
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,833
Well, I talked it over with Cecily,

259
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,992
and we decided to change our
flight reservations to Christmas Eve.

260
00:17:11,160 --> 00:17:13,037
Why?

261
00:17:13,200 --> 00:17:15,316
The feeling most commonly associated
with Christmas:

262
00:17:15,480 --> 00:17:17,038
Guilt.

263
00:17:18,680 --> 00:17:21,148
- You too?
- Yeah, me too.

264
00:17:21,600 --> 00:17:23,033
Strong motivation.

265
00:17:24,960 --> 00:17:26,712
I checked the evidence
in police property,

266
00:17:26,880 --> 00:17:29,314
and McCabe's fingerprints
are all over the letter opener.

267
00:17:30,120 --> 00:17:31,872
I wonder why
they weren't on the paperweight

268
00:17:32,040 --> 00:17:33,996
that was used to bludgeon Rollins.

269
00:17:34,160 --> 00:17:36,594
Tom McCabe said he struggled
with his attacker,

270
00:17:36,760 --> 00:17:39,991
and that would explain how his prints
got on the letter opener.

271
00:17:40,160 --> 00:17:43,072
Well, then, how did it get back
into Rollins' office?

272
00:17:43,240 --> 00:17:45,913
All right. I've talked to Rollins'
secretary, and according to her,

273
00:17:46,080 --> 00:17:48,196
besides herself, there was only
three other people

274
00:17:48,360 --> 00:17:51,352
that were in the office the next morning
that had a chance to bring it back.

275
00:17:51,520 --> 00:17:53,988
And that would be Polson,
Harris, and Lang.

276
00:17:54,840 --> 00:17:56,398
Think it must be one of those three?

277
00:17:56,960 --> 00:17:58,678
Place to start.

278
00:17:58,840 --> 00:18:01,308
This is a very lovely bracelet.

279
00:18:01,480 --> 00:18:03,710
The diamonds and sapphires
are perfectly matched

280
00:18:03,880 --> 00:18:05,757
in a platinum setting.

281
00:18:05,920 --> 00:18:07,911
- Pretty.
- Yes, it's one of a kind.

282
00:18:08,080 --> 00:18:10,310
Oh, really? That's too bad.

283
00:18:10,480 --> 00:18:12,550
See, I need three of them. Identical.

284
00:18:12,720 --> 00:18:14,039
Oh, I see.

285
00:18:14,200 --> 00:18:17,875
Well, I think I might have
precisely what you're looking for.

286
00:18:18,040 --> 00:18:19,837
Good. Thank you.

287
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:21,913
Three identical bracelets?

288
00:18:22,080 --> 00:18:24,913
So you don't have to remember
which lady got what?

289
00:18:25,080 --> 00:18:28,152
- Who are you?
- Charlene Matlock.

290
00:18:28,320 --> 00:18:30,436
Dan Polson. Nice to meet you.

291
00:18:30,600 --> 00:18:32,830
I'm an attorney
representing Mr. McCabe.

292
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:34,831
Your assistant told me
I could find you in here

293
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:36,274
doing some Christmas shopping.

294
00:18:36,440 --> 00:18:38,271
A real bloodhound, aren't you?

295
00:18:38,440 --> 00:18:41,750
I wanted to ask you where you were
when Rollins was killed.

296
00:18:42,360 --> 00:18:44,715
- You think I did it?
- I didn't say that.

297
00:18:44,880 --> 00:18:47,997
But you are executive vice-president
of this whole shooting match, and...

298
00:18:49,200 --> 00:18:52,317
Well, now with Rollins gone,
you are next in line to take over.

299
00:18:53,040 --> 00:18:56,032
Believe me, I outmanoeuvre people
to get ahead in business.

300
00:18:56,200 --> 00:18:57,679
I don't murder them.

301
00:18:58,720 --> 00:19:00,358
You still haven't answered
my question.

302
00:19:00,800 --> 00:19:03,314
Oh, yes. You want my alibi.

303
00:19:03,480 --> 00:19:06,552
I was home alone.

304
00:19:06,720 --> 00:19:07,709
Tell you what,

305
00:19:07,880 --> 00:19:10,599
let me finish my shopping,
and then we'll have dinner tonight

306
00:19:10,760 --> 00:19:13,672
and you can check me out to your
heart's content. What do you say?

307
00:19:14,280 --> 00:19:16,840
Let's make it lunch.
Tomorrow, and I'll pick the place.

308
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:18,558
Perfect.

309
00:19:18,720 --> 00:19:21,109
Alright. I'll call you in your office
in the morning.

310
00:19:21,280 --> 00:19:23,032
Fine.

311
00:19:27,600 --> 00:19:29,192
<i>- Rollins here.</i>
<i>- Alan?</i>

312
00:19:29,360 --> 00:19:31,669
<i>- It's Mark.</i>
<i>- Oh, hi, Mark. How you doing?</i>

313
00:19:31,840 --> 00:19:33,068
<i>How's everything in Savannah?</i>

314
00:19:33,240 --> 00:19:36,038
<i>Well, we're still a few days behind</i>
<i>because of that faulty scaffolding,</i>

315
00:19:36,200 --> 00:19:37,394
<i>but we're catching up.</i>

316
00:19:37,560 --> 00:19:39,596
<i>What about you?</i>
<i>How'd the eviction go?</i>

317
00:19:39,760 --> 00:19:43,389
<i>Yeah, well, it wasn't exactly a public</i>
<i>relations triumph, I can tell you that.</i>

318
00:19:43,560 --> 00:19:46,711
<i>- Triumph? Sounds to me like--</i>
- I'm Mark Harris.

319
00:19:46,880 --> 00:19:48,029
<i>--headlines read/ ''Scrooge--</i>

320
00:19:48,200 --> 00:19:50,668
That's me on the phone
with Mr. Rollins.

321
00:19:54,120 --> 00:19:56,680
You were in Savannah
the night of the murder.

322
00:19:56,840 --> 00:19:57,909
Right.

323
00:19:58,080 --> 00:19:59,798
What were you doing in Savannah,
Mr. Harris?

324
00:19:59,960 --> 00:20:01,951
- If you don't mind my asking.
- No, not at all.

325
00:20:02,120 --> 00:20:03,553
We're building
a shopping mall there,

326
00:20:03,720 --> 00:20:05,711
and I spend a couple of days a week
on the site.

327
00:20:05,880 --> 00:20:08,792
It's one of the less thrilling aspects
of my job.

328
00:20:09,400 --> 00:20:13,313
Listen, this is a little creepy.
Could we talk in my office?

329
00:20:13,840 --> 00:20:15,398
Sure.

330
00:20:15,560 --> 00:20:17,835
Where do you stay
when you're in Savannah?

331
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:21,117
The corporation has an apartment
near the site.

332
00:20:21,280 --> 00:20:25,159
Is that where you were the night
that you spoke with Mr. Rollins?

333
00:20:25,840 --> 00:20:28,195
Just a few minutes
before he was killed, I understand.

334
00:20:28,360 --> 00:20:29,679
Anybody see you there?

335
00:20:29,840 --> 00:20:33,992
Afraid not. I went to bed early. Alone.

336
00:20:35,960 --> 00:20:38,474
That call came from there. You can
check with the phone company.

337
00:20:38,640 --> 00:20:39,629
I'll do that.

338
00:20:39,800 --> 00:20:41,597
- I wouldn't want you to think--
- Mark?

339
00:20:43,480 --> 00:20:46,870
- Oh, excuse me.
- This is my wife, Annie.

340
00:20:47,040 --> 00:20:48,598
Ben Matlock.

341
00:20:48,760 --> 00:20:51,638
Mr. Matlock's defending the man
who's accused of murdering Alan.

342
00:20:51,800 --> 00:20:53,631
Well, what's he doing here?

343
00:20:53,800 --> 00:20:57,156
Oh, well, I'll be leaving.
Nice to have met you, Mrs. Harris.

344
00:20:57,320 --> 00:20:58,753
We'll talk.

345
00:21:12,440 --> 00:21:13,429
Good game.

346
00:21:17,880 --> 00:21:20,838
- Mr. Lang?
- That's right.

347
00:21:21,400 --> 00:21:23,550
It's a terrible shame
what happened to Mr. Rollins.

348
00:21:24,440 --> 00:21:26,874
- Think so?
- Well, don't you?

349
00:21:27,040 --> 00:21:28,189
Oh, maybe you don't.

350
00:21:28,360 --> 00:21:30,828
After all that bad publicity
over the eviction,

351
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:32,831
you must've been worried
about unemployment.

352
00:21:34,640 --> 00:21:37,632
You're real cute.
Anybody ever tell you that?

353
00:21:37,800 --> 00:21:40,917
No, but they told me that you were
one of the people that found his body.

354
00:21:41,080 --> 00:21:45,119
Listen, I already gave a statement
to the homicide detective.

355
00:21:45,280 --> 00:21:47,510
Well, I'm not a cop.

356
00:21:47,680 --> 00:21:50,353
I'm a private investigator and I'm
working on behalf of Mr. McCabe.

357
00:21:50,520 --> 00:21:52,954
- Yeah? Well, who's he?
- He's the one the cops think did it.

358
00:21:53,120 --> 00:21:54,872
Oh, and you're here
trying to prove he didn't?

359
00:21:55,040 --> 00:21:57,076
I just wanna find out
what is happening.

360
00:21:57,880 --> 00:21:59,950
Good luck. Excuse me.

361
00:22:00,120 --> 00:22:04,318
Well, where were you that night
about 9:00?

362
00:22:04,480 --> 00:22:06,550
I was here playing basketball.

363
00:22:06,720 --> 00:22:08,950
And your buddies
will back you up on that, of course.

364
00:22:09,560 --> 00:22:10,754
Sure.

365
00:22:10,920 --> 00:22:12,672
Now beat it.

366
00:22:15,240 --> 00:22:18,755
- I'll talk to you again.
- I can hardly wait.

367
00:23:02,560 --> 00:23:04,471
Where'd everybody go?

368
00:23:57,560 --> 00:23:59,278
- Well, we better go.
- Okay.

369
00:23:59,440 --> 00:24:00,953
Thanks.

370
00:24:02,640 --> 00:24:03,834
- Where have you been?
- Tyler.

371
00:24:05,040 --> 00:24:07,918
I spent the night in a gymnasium,
that's where I've been.

372
00:24:08,080 --> 00:24:10,958
Why don't you try spending the night
at home once in a while?

373
00:24:11,680 --> 00:24:13,910
Age hasn't softened you, has it?

374
00:24:14,080 --> 00:24:15,115
Carl Lang ditched me.

375
00:24:15,280 --> 00:24:17,111
He and his buddies
locked me in a gym.

376
00:24:17,280 --> 00:24:20,317
Now, I could've sat there, Ionely,
mad as hell at you

377
00:24:20,480 --> 00:24:23,631
for getting me back
into this godforsaken business, but no.

378
00:24:23,800 --> 00:24:26,872
Being the highly-trained,
resourceful operative that I am,

379
00:24:27,040 --> 00:24:29,679
I turned the night into an advantage.

380
00:24:32,280 --> 00:24:34,111
- What did you find out?
- Lang lied.

381
00:24:34,280 --> 00:24:36,430
He wasn't shooting hoops
the night of the murder.

382
00:24:36,600 --> 00:24:39,637
I'd found the records of the game.
He wasn't there.

383
00:24:39,800 --> 00:24:43,759
- Where was he?
- I don't know yet.

384
00:24:44,880 --> 00:24:48,429
Well, he might be our man.
Stay with him.

385
00:24:48,600 --> 00:24:51,273
Tyler, I'm...

386
00:24:51,440 --> 00:24:54,876
I'm sorry
about the night in the gymnasium.

387
00:24:55,040 --> 00:24:57,474
See, I have softened some.

388
00:25:05,360 --> 00:25:06,634
- Great.
- Merry Christmas.

389
00:25:06,800 --> 00:25:08,074
- Same to you.
- Merry Christmas.

390
00:25:08,240 --> 00:25:10,549
I'm making veal piccata.
I hope you like it.

391
00:25:10,720 --> 00:25:12,676
- Terrific.
- Super.

392
00:25:13,080 --> 00:25:15,878
Oh, that's Mr. Ferguson up in 3A.

393
00:25:16,040 --> 00:25:18,349
He turned on his water.

394
00:25:22,560 --> 00:25:24,835
Don't you think you're overdoing this
just a bit?

395
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:26,831
It's lunch. My choice.

396
00:25:27,960 --> 00:25:29,598
Who's serving dessert? Tiny Tim?

397
00:25:31,040 --> 00:25:33,076
Here we are. I hope you like it.

398
00:25:33,240 --> 00:25:35,037
Great.

399
00:25:36,440 --> 00:25:38,317
You know, this is very nouvelle.

400
00:25:40,200 --> 00:25:41,918
It's all they can afford.

401
00:25:45,680 --> 00:25:48,114
- It's delicious.
- Oh, thank you.

402
00:25:49,680 --> 00:25:51,352
Cold, isn't it?

403
00:25:51,520 --> 00:25:53,875
Mrs. Spellman,
would you mind turning up the heat?

404
00:25:54,040 --> 00:25:55,155
What heat?

405
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,468
Okay, okay. I get the point.

406
00:26:00,920 --> 00:26:03,229
- Can we go now?
- One more stop.

407
00:26:03,400 --> 00:26:05,675
Mrs. Ross' apartment upstairs, 2B.

408
00:26:05,840 --> 00:26:07,273
You go ahead. The door's open.

409
00:26:07,440 --> 00:26:09,396
I'll just stay
and thank Mrs. Spellman.

410
00:26:09,560 --> 00:26:11,232
Then can we go to lunch?

411
00:26:14,800 --> 00:26:17,189
- Thanks again.
- Oh, anytime.

412
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:42,956
Now what?

413
00:26:43,120 --> 00:26:46,078
You don't have an alibi for the night
of the murder, Mr. Polson.

414
00:26:46,240 --> 00:26:49,596
We had to know if you'd ever
been in Mr. McCabe's apartment.

415
00:26:49,760 --> 00:26:51,318
- I wasn't.
- I know.

416
00:26:51,480 --> 00:26:55,029
Whoever the killer was knows where
the light switch is, and you don't.

417
00:26:58,400 --> 00:27:00,311
- God bless you.
- Thank you.

418
00:27:00,880 --> 00:27:02,233
That's some cold you got.

419
00:27:03,360 --> 00:27:06,716
Oh, it's not a cold.
I'm allergic to cats.

420
00:27:10,120 --> 00:27:12,076
Are you satisfied?

421
00:27:12,840 --> 00:27:13,829
Excuse me.

422
00:27:17,840 --> 00:27:21,879
Jonathan,
I've got to go to work, old boy.

423
00:27:22,600 --> 00:27:24,192
Stay there.

424
00:27:25,160 --> 00:27:27,151
You travel light, I'll say that.

425
00:27:27,320 --> 00:27:28,878
What do you mean?

426
00:27:29,720 --> 00:27:31,711
There's no pictures.

427
00:27:33,800 --> 00:27:36,519
I've got nothing much
I wanna remember.

428
00:27:37,880 --> 00:27:39,598
You know, I knew a fellow once.

429
00:27:39,760 --> 00:27:41,716
He was a welder.

430
00:27:41,880 --> 00:27:44,713
He goes off to Korea
and gets himself all shot up.

431
00:27:44,880 --> 00:27:46,996
So when he comes home,
he can't get a job.

432
00:27:47,600 --> 00:27:50,239
Wife runs off with another man.

433
00:27:50,400 --> 00:27:52,630
You know what he does
to answer his problems?

434
00:27:55,080 --> 00:27:56,752
Robs a bank.

435
00:27:58,240 --> 00:28:01,038
So I've got a record. So what?

436
00:28:01,200 --> 00:28:03,316
What's your point, Matlock?

437
00:28:03,480 --> 00:28:06,950
The point is, feeling sorry for yourself
will get you in trouble.

438
00:28:07,480 --> 00:28:09,357
I don't feel sorry for myself.

439
00:28:09,800 --> 00:28:11,518
I take the world as it comes.

440
00:28:11,680 --> 00:28:12,829
How's that?

441
00:28:13,000 --> 00:28:16,993
Nobody does anything for anyone
but themselves.

442
00:28:17,160 --> 00:28:18,991
See you in a little bit.

443
00:28:24,840 --> 00:28:28,310
I've got to tell you something, Tom,
and I don't mean to disillusion you.

444
00:28:28,480 --> 00:28:31,278
I'm not getting rich off your case.

445
00:28:31,440 --> 00:28:33,795
I'm your bit for society, Ben.

446
00:28:33,960 --> 00:28:36,474
A little charity's good for the soul.

447
00:28:39,520 --> 00:28:41,875
I'll take over from here, sir.

448
00:28:43,040 --> 00:28:45,838
That's right, Tom.
I do this every year.

449
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:49,788
I go out and find the nastiest,
hatefulest, meanest person in town

450
00:28:49,960 --> 00:28:52,349
and I represent him.
Makes me feel good.

451
00:28:52,520 --> 00:28:53,919
This year, you're it.

452
00:28:55,400 --> 00:28:57,755
Merry Christmas,
and I'll see you in court.

453
00:29:04,720 --> 00:29:08,269
Lieutenant Brooks,
I show you People's Exhibit 4.

454
00:29:08,440 --> 00:29:10,795
And I ask if you recognise it.

455
00:29:10,960 --> 00:29:13,633
- Yes. It has my initials.
- Where was it found?

456
00:29:13,800 --> 00:29:15,472
On the floor of Alan Rollins' office.

457
00:29:15,640 --> 00:29:17,870
- Were any fingerprints found on it?
- Yes.

458
00:29:18,040 --> 00:29:19,109
Anything else?

459
00:29:19,280 --> 00:29:22,352
Yes, traces of what appeared to be
dried blood.

460
00:29:22,960 --> 00:29:25,554
Your Honour, for the purpose
of this preliminary hearing,

461
00:29:25,720 --> 00:29:28,439
the defence counsel has already
stipulated that the fingerprints found

462
00:29:28,600 --> 00:29:30,318
match those of the defendant.

463
00:29:31,240 --> 00:29:34,357
And that type-O bloodstains
were identified by Forensic

464
00:29:34,520 --> 00:29:36,670
to be on the weapon.

465
00:29:37,880 --> 00:29:42,510
Defendant's blood type has previously
been admitted to be type-O as well.

466
00:29:43,040 --> 00:29:48,034
Lieutenant Brooks, I show you
a legal file of eviction notices,

467
00:29:48,200 --> 00:29:50,794
marked People's Exhibit 9,

468
00:29:50,960 --> 00:29:54,316
and identified as being missing
from the deceased's office.

469
00:29:54,480 --> 00:29:56,436
- Did you find these documents?
- Yes, I did.

470
00:29:56,600 --> 00:29:59,273
- Where?
- In Mr. McCabe's apartment.

471
00:29:59,440 --> 00:30:01,078
Thank you.

472
00:30:01,400 --> 00:30:03,834
I've no further questions
of this witness, Your Honour.

473
00:30:05,280 --> 00:30:07,316
Mr. Matlock?

474
00:30:07,480 --> 00:30:09,277
No questions, Your Honour.

475
00:30:10,200 --> 00:30:11,269
You may step down.

476
00:30:11,440 --> 00:30:14,591
Your Honour,
I call Mr. Duke Mitchell to the stand.

477
00:30:16,560 --> 00:30:18,710
Mr. Mitchell, you're the security guard
in the building

478
00:30:18,880 --> 00:30:21,314
where Alan Rollins has
his corporate offices. Is that correct?

479
00:30:21,480 --> 00:30:22,708
Yes, sir, it is.

480
00:30:22,880 --> 00:30:25,235
And you were on duty in the lobby
the night of the murder?

481
00:30:25,400 --> 00:30:26,435
Yes, sir, I was.

482
00:30:26,600 --> 00:30:30,036
At approximately 8:50 that night,
did someone enter the building

483
00:30:30,200 --> 00:30:32,760
- and go up to Alan Rollins' offices?
- Yes, sir.

484
00:30:32,920 --> 00:30:35,070
Was that person
dressed as Santa Claus?

485
00:30:35,240 --> 00:30:36,593
Yes, sir.

486
00:30:38,120 --> 00:30:40,395
It's probably tough
to tell one Santa Claus

487
00:30:40,560 --> 00:30:42,357
- from another Santa Claus.
- Yes, sir.

488
00:30:42,520 --> 00:30:45,990
So you probably couldn't give
a specific identification, could you?

489
00:30:46,160 --> 00:30:48,071
No, sir, I could not.

490
00:30:48,240 --> 00:30:52,472
But did you notice anything special
about this particular Santa Claus?

491
00:30:52,640 --> 00:30:54,153
- Yes.
- What was it?

492
00:30:54,320 --> 00:30:55,355
He limped.

493
00:30:55,840 --> 00:30:57,990
Your Honour, at this time
the state requests

494
00:30:58,160 --> 00:31:00,594
that the court take judicial notice

495
00:31:00,760 --> 00:31:05,470
that the defendant, Mr. Tom McCabe,
walks with a conspicuous limp.

496
00:31:05,920 --> 00:31:07,797
No objection, Your Honour. It's fact.

497
00:31:07,960 --> 00:31:10,155
No further questions.

498
00:31:10,320 --> 00:31:12,151
Cross-examine?

499
00:31:13,600 --> 00:31:15,477
Mr. Mitchell.

500
00:31:16,240 --> 00:31:18,356
I congratulate you

501
00:31:18,520 --> 00:31:20,272
on your powers of observation.

502
00:31:20,440 --> 00:31:22,192
Thank you, sir.

503
00:31:22,960 --> 00:31:26,714
I imagine it gets pretty quiet down in
that lobby after everybody goes home.

504
00:31:26,880 --> 00:31:28,313
Oh, yeah.

505
00:31:28,800 --> 00:31:32,190
So I imagine you pay particular
attention when somebody comes in.

506
00:31:32,360 --> 00:31:33,349
Oh, that I do.

507
00:31:33,520 --> 00:31:36,239
Enough attention to tell us
which leg Santa Claus limped on?

508
00:31:36,400 --> 00:31:38,118
Oh, yes. It was his right leg.

509
00:31:38,280 --> 00:31:40,919
Oh, kind of like this:

510
00:31:42,280 --> 00:31:43,759
Oh, yes, sir. Exactly like that.

511
00:31:44,440 --> 00:31:46,317
This is working out real good.

512
00:31:46,480 --> 00:31:50,871
Mr. McCabe, would you please stand
and walk across the courtroom?

513
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:01,069
Good.

514
00:32:04,520 --> 00:32:06,238
That's good.

515
00:32:06,560 --> 00:32:09,120
Now, did the man you see
the night of the murder

516
00:32:09,280 --> 00:32:11,874
limp on the same leg
as Mr. McCabe?

517
00:32:12,480 --> 00:32:13,595
Objection, Your Honour.

518
00:32:14,120 --> 00:32:16,680
It's an improper demonstration.
Anyone can fake a limp.

519
00:32:16,840 --> 00:32:18,558
That's right, Your Honour,
and someone did.

520
00:32:18,720 --> 00:32:20,790
But not Mr. McCabe.

521
00:32:22,840 --> 00:32:24,751
At this time,

522
00:32:25,400 --> 00:32:29,518
I'd like to place into evidence
these medical records

523
00:32:29,680 --> 00:32:33,639
which show that in 1 952,
in the Korean War,

524
00:32:33,800 --> 00:32:38,078
Mr. McCabe received
several shrapnel wounds in his left leg

525
00:32:38,240 --> 00:32:39,992
and has been disabled ever since.

526
00:32:40,160 --> 00:32:43,152
- Exhibit is received in evidence.
- Thank you, Your Honour.

527
00:32:43,320 --> 00:32:45,038
Mr. Mitchell, I ask you again:

528
00:32:46,440 --> 00:32:48,192
Did the man you saw
the night of the murder

529
00:32:48,360 --> 00:32:50,794
limp on the same leg
as Mr. McCabe?

530
00:32:51,240 --> 00:32:52,912
No, sir. He did not.

531
00:32:53,760 --> 00:32:56,558
Thank you, Mr. Mitchell,
for being an honest man.

532
00:32:56,720 --> 00:32:58,073
No further questions.

533
00:32:59,440 --> 00:33:01,237
You may step down.

534
00:33:05,400 --> 00:33:07,868
Your Honour, new evidence
has just come to our attention.

535
00:33:08,040 --> 00:33:10,235
We beg the court's indulgence
for a recess.

536
00:33:10,400 --> 00:33:11,515
Very well.

537
00:33:11,680 --> 00:33:14,752
Court will reconvene
at 9 a.m. tomorrow.

538
00:33:21,960 --> 00:33:24,520
- Mr. Lang.
- You again.

539
00:33:24,680 --> 00:33:27,148
Me again.
And this time, I brought my attorney.

540
00:33:27,320 --> 00:33:29,675
Ben Matlock, Carl Lang.

541
00:33:30,880 --> 00:33:31,995
May we come in?

542
00:33:35,120 --> 00:33:36,348
Of course.

543
00:33:38,760 --> 00:33:42,833
Man, this must cost
an arm and a leg.

544
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:44,877
How many bedrooms?

545
00:33:45,040 --> 00:33:46,951
Just one.

546
00:33:47,880 --> 00:33:51,634
How about we skip the small talk
and cut straight to the chase?

547
00:33:51,800 --> 00:33:53,870
- Get to the point.
- Oh, okay.

548
00:33:54,040 --> 00:33:58,477
Mr. Lang, whoever killed Mr. Rollins
framed my client

549
00:33:58,640 --> 00:34:03,509
by planting the letter opener
in the office before the police arrived.

550
00:34:03,680 --> 00:34:09,710
Now, the guard said that nobody else
entered the building that night.

551
00:34:09,880 --> 00:34:13,156
So it had to have been done
the next morning.

552
00:34:13,320 --> 00:34:15,072
You were one of the people
who found him.

553
00:34:15,240 --> 00:34:17,435
You had the opportunity,

554
00:34:17,600 --> 00:34:20,956
since you weren't playing basketball
the night of the murder.

555
00:34:21,120 --> 00:34:22,951
You lied about your whereabouts.

556
00:34:23,120 --> 00:34:24,917
So I lied.

557
00:34:25,640 --> 00:34:29,155
Can Mrs. Harris
supply you with an alibi?

558
00:34:33,240 --> 00:34:34,992
He was with me.

559
00:34:35,160 --> 00:34:36,798
Mrs. Harris,

560
00:34:38,120 --> 00:34:42,477
this is Tyler Hudson, an investigator.
He saw you arrive earlier.

561
00:34:42,640 --> 00:34:46,394
You'll testify that you were here
with Mr. Lang the night of the murder?

562
00:34:46,560 --> 00:34:48,915
- Yes.
- Leave her out of this.

563
00:34:49,080 --> 00:34:51,196
How long has this relationship
been going on?

564
00:34:51,360 --> 00:34:53,191
I said leave her out of this.

565
00:34:53,360 --> 00:34:54,679
Almost a year.

566
00:34:55,200 --> 00:34:58,237
Ever since
Mark started going to Savannah.

567
00:34:58,400 --> 00:35:01,472
- Does he know?
- He knows something, I guess.

568
00:35:01,640 --> 00:35:03,949
He's been questioning all my friends.

569
00:35:04,120 --> 00:35:06,111
He has to know.

570
00:35:06,360 --> 00:35:07,395
Does he know who?

571
00:35:08,680 --> 00:35:09,749
No.

572
00:35:12,920 --> 00:35:17,357
Well, I know you have
a million things to do, so we'll go.

573
00:35:23,680 --> 00:35:27,559
- A million things to do, huh?
- Yeah, well, Christmas shopping.

574
00:35:28,760 --> 00:35:30,159
You know, it could be

575
00:35:30,320 --> 00:35:34,074
Mark Harris thought Alan Rollins
was seeing his wife.

576
00:35:34,240 --> 00:35:37,437
Mr. Rollins had a reputation
as a player.

577
00:35:37,600 --> 00:35:40,068
He's the one who sent Mr. Harris
out of town every week.

578
00:35:40,240 --> 00:35:43,710
Yeah, well, that's a nice motive, but
he was supposed to be in Savannah.

579
00:35:43,880 --> 00:35:46,997
That's right.
He was supposed to be in Savannah.

580
00:36:01,640 --> 00:36:03,676
- Thinking?
- I'm waiting.

581
00:36:03,840 --> 00:36:05,353
For what?

582
00:36:06,960 --> 00:36:08,188
- Hello?
<i>- Ben?</i>

583
00:36:08,760 --> 00:36:11,479
<i>- You in Savannah?</i>
- Yes.

584
00:36:11,800 --> 00:36:13,074
This'll just take a minute.

585
00:36:21,680 --> 00:36:24,478
Sarah, would you get me Tyler
at that number in Savannah?

586
00:36:24,640 --> 00:36:26,153
All right.

587
00:36:26,320 --> 00:36:28,834
- What are you up to?
- Watch.

588
00:36:31,600 --> 00:36:33,079
It's ringing.

589
00:36:35,640 --> 00:36:38,359
Charlene, that's your private line.

590
00:36:41,840 --> 00:36:44,957
- Hello?
<i>- Charlene?</i>

591
00:36:49,160 --> 00:36:50,275
Dad?

592
00:36:51,400 --> 00:36:53,436
I think we got him.

593
00:36:56,160 --> 00:36:58,037
Mr. Harris,

594
00:36:58,200 --> 00:37:00,475
you're currently
supervising the construction

595
00:37:00,640 --> 00:37:04,474
of a shopping centre in Savannah and
you go there on Wednesday nights.

596
00:37:04,640 --> 00:37:06,119
- Is that correct?
- Yes.

597
00:37:06,280 --> 00:37:09,272
So you were in Savannah,
in the corporation's apartment,

598
00:37:09,440 --> 00:37:11,510
the night that Alan Rollins
was murdered.

599
00:37:11,680 --> 00:37:12,669
Yes.

600
00:37:12,840 --> 00:37:15,877
In fact, you placed a call to Mr. Rollins
from that apartment that night.

601
00:37:16,040 --> 00:37:17,029
Yes.

602
00:37:17,200 --> 00:37:20,112
And you knew the call was taped
because Mr. Rollins taped all his calls.

603
00:37:20,280 --> 00:37:21,429
Yes, I did know that.

604
00:37:22,680 --> 00:37:24,432
Your Honour, at this time,

605
00:37:24,600 --> 00:37:29,833
I would like the actual tape recording
of that conversation

606
00:37:30,000 --> 00:37:33,549
marked Defendant Exhibit G
for identification.

607
00:37:34,000 --> 00:37:35,228
It'll be marked.

608
00:37:36,600 --> 00:37:40,070
Mr. Harris, did you place any other
calls from Savannah that night?

609
00:37:40,240 --> 00:37:43,232
- Did you call your wife?
- No, I did not.

610
00:37:43,640 --> 00:37:44,993
Any particular reason?

611
00:37:45,840 --> 00:37:47,068
She wouldn't have been at home.

612
00:37:47,240 --> 00:37:49,834
My wife is taking an extension course
at Baxter University.

613
00:37:50,000 --> 00:37:51,638
She attends classes
Wednesday nights

614
00:37:51,800 --> 00:37:53,392
because that's the night
I'm out of town.

615
00:37:55,000 --> 00:37:57,116
Would Miss Ruth Bennett
please stand?

616
00:37:59,360 --> 00:38:03,319
- You recognise that young lady?
- Yes.

617
00:38:03,480 --> 00:38:05,516
Isn't it true she's a fellow student
of your wife's

618
00:38:05,680 --> 00:38:07,272
and you met with her a month ago

619
00:38:07,440 --> 00:38:09,635
to discuss your wife's attendance
to class?

620
00:38:10,440 --> 00:38:11,475
Yes.

621
00:38:11,640 --> 00:38:15,394
And your wife has missed
quite a few classes, true?

622
00:38:17,880 --> 00:38:18,869
Yes.

623
00:38:19,040 --> 00:38:21,759
Actually, you learned that your wife
has not attended any classes,

624
00:38:21,920 --> 00:38:24,036
Wednesday night
or any other night, true?

625
00:38:24,200 --> 00:38:26,668
Objection. Calls for hearsay.

626
00:38:27,160 --> 00:38:28,434
Overruled.

627
00:38:29,000 --> 00:38:31,195
It's not for the truth,
but what he heard.

628
00:38:32,200 --> 00:38:35,272
- You may answer.
- Yes, that's true.

629
00:38:35,440 --> 00:38:40,434
Mr. Harris, we're talking about
a rather beautiful woman here,

630
00:38:40,880 --> 00:38:43,917
who's been lying to her husband
about her whereabouts.

631
00:38:44,520 --> 00:38:47,671
Isn't it true
you thought she was having an affair?

632
00:38:47,880 --> 00:38:49,359
- No.
- But wasn't it your employer,

633
00:38:49,520 --> 00:38:52,956
Mr. Rollins, who sent you out of town
to Savannah every week?

634
00:38:53,120 --> 00:38:54,348
Yes.

635
00:38:54,520 --> 00:38:57,478
And wasn't Mr. Rollins
frequently out of his own office

636
00:38:57,640 --> 00:38:58,914
on Wednesday nights?

637
00:38:59,080 --> 00:39:00,229
I wouldn't know.

638
00:39:00,400 --> 00:39:02,311
And didn't Mr. Rollins
have a reputation

639
00:39:02,480 --> 00:39:04,550
of being something of a ladies' man?

640
00:39:04,720 --> 00:39:05,709
I don't know about that.

641
00:39:05,880 --> 00:39:07,871
Didn't you
draw the obvious conclusion

642
00:39:08,040 --> 00:39:10,508
that your wife was having an affair
with Alan Rollins?

643
00:39:10,680 --> 00:39:12,352
- No, I did not.
- Objection.

644
00:39:12,800 --> 00:39:15,598
- Relevancy.
- Sustained.

645
00:39:17,320 --> 00:39:19,436
For what it's worth,

646
00:39:19,600 --> 00:39:22,592
your wife was not having an affair
with Alan Rollins.

647
00:39:22,760 --> 00:39:24,716
She was having an affair
with Carl Lang.

648
00:39:25,320 --> 00:39:26,673
Objection.

649
00:39:26,840 --> 00:39:29,673
Mrs. Harris' love life is completely
irrelevant to the case at hand.

650
00:39:29,840 --> 00:39:31,478
I think it's motive, Your Honour.

651
00:39:31,640 --> 00:39:34,677
I think it's motive for a jealous husband
to commit murder

652
00:39:34,840 --> 00:39:38,355
on the mistaken belief that his wife
was sleeping with the victim.

653
00:39:38,960 --> 00:39:40,234
Sustained.

654
00:39:41,800 --> 00:39:44,553
Mr. Harris, the night of the murder,

655
00:39:44,720 --> 00:39:49,271
you knew Alan Rollins had to be in
his office preparing documents

656
00:39:49,440 --> 00:39:51,908
for a very important meeting
the next day, did you not?

657
00:39:52,080 --> 00:39:53,069
I suppose so.

658
00:39:53,240 --> 00:39:56,596
But you contend you were
in Savannah the night of the murder,

659
00:39:56,760 --> 00:40:00,150
and that you called Alan Rollins
from the corporation's apartment.

660
00:40:00,320 --> 00:40:01,753
Yes.

661
00:40:03,560 --> 00:40:05,471
This is a bill

662
00:40:05,640 --> 00:40:08,234
from that phone in Savannah.

663
00:40:08,400 --> 00:40:12,871
And, as you say,
a call was placed that night

664
00:40:13,040 --> 00:40:15,759
to Mr. Rollins in his office in Atlanta.

665
00:40:15,920 --> 00:40:18,559
Made at one minute to 9,
lasting almost three minutes

666
00:40:18,720 --> 00:40:21,314
at a cost of seventy-four cents.

667
00:40:22,720 --> 00:40:24,631
Now, that had me stumped
for a while, Mr. Harris.

668
00:40:24,800 --> 00:40:26,631
I couldn't figure it out.

669
00:40:27,240 --> 00:40:30,710
And then I remembered
call forwarding.

670
00:40:31,200 --> 00:40:34,636
It's one of those marvellous services
the phone company offers

671
00:40:34,800 --> 00:40:39,669
that permits subscribers to place a call
from one location

672
00:40:39,840 --> 00:40:42,115
and have it sent through another.

673
00:40:43,200 --> 00:40:46,875
So I checked the bill from your
private phone in your office in Atlanta,

674
00:40:47,320 --> 00:40:50,710
which is just down the hall
from Mr. Rollins.

675
00:40:51,360 --> 00:40:53,669
And a call was placed
from that number,

676
00:40:53,840 --> 00:40:57,355
forwarded through the number
in Savannah,

677
00:40:57,840 --> 00:41:01,037
at one minute to 9,
lasting almost three minutes,

678
00:41:01,200 --> 00:41:03,760
at a cost of seventy-four cents.

679
00:41:05,000 --> 00:41:07,560
Your Honour,
the defence offers that tape,

680
00:41:08,360 --> 00:41:11,033
Defendant Exhibit G,
into evidence

681
00:41:11,200 --> 00:41:13,509
and ask that it be played.

682
00:41:13,880 --> 00:41:15,313
Exhibit received in evidence.

683
00:41:15,760 --> 00:41:16,795
Proceed.

684
00:41:18,600 --> 00:41:20,909
Now, Mr. Harris,

685
00:41:21,080 --> 00:41:22,354
what do you hear?

686
00:41:22,520 --> 00:41:24,192
<i>What about you?</i>
<i>How'd the eviction go?</i>

687
00:41:24,360 --> 00:41:26,396
<i>Yeah, well, it wasn't exactly</i>
<i>a public relations--</i>

688
00:41:26,560 --> 00:41:28,471
My voice. Alan's.

689
00:41:28,640 --> 00:41:30,198
Anything else?

690
00:41:31,560 --> 00:41:35,633
- No.
- Let's listen again, a little bit louder.

691
00:41:37,040 --> 00:41:38,519
- Okay.
<i>--the eviction go?</i>

692
00:41:38,680 --> 00:41:40,636
<i>Yeah, well, it wasn't exactly</i>
<i>a public relations--</i>

693
00:41:40,800 --> 00:41:44,190
Do you hear that?
What is that in the background?

694
00:41:44,360 --> 00:41:45,759
I don't know.

695
00:41:45,920 --> 00:41:50,630
Well, let's listen to it one more time,
a little louder still.

696
00:41:50,800 --> 00:41:51,915
<i>--the eviction go?</i>

697
00:41:52,080 --> 00:41:54,310
<i>Yeah, well, it wasn't exactly</i>
<i>a public relations triumph--</i>

698
00:41:54,480 --> 00:41:56,152
You hear that?

699
00:41:56,320 --> 00:42:00,552
Sounds like a clock chiming to me.
That what it sounds like to you?

700
00:42:01,280 --> 00:42:02,838
I suppose so, yes.

701
00:42:04,800 --> 00:42:08,156
Now, Mr. Harris.

702
00:42:12,240 --> 00:42:14,595
Do you recognise this clock?

703
00:42:15,560 --> 00:42:18,120
The clock's from your office, isn't it?

704
00:42:20,600 --> 00:42:21,589
Yes.

705
00:42:21,760 --> 00:42:25,150
Isn't it true you placed that call
from your office?

706
00:42:25,320 --> 00:42:28,676
Isn't it true you set the phone
in Savannah

707
00:42:28,840 --> 00:42:32,992
to forward all calls
to Alan Rollins' private line,

708
00:42:33,640 --> 00:42:37,679
and after you spoke with him for a few
moments from your office in Atlanta,

709
00:42:37,840 --> 00:42:41,116
forwarded through the phone
in Savannah,

710
00:42:41,280 --> 00:42:45,068
you walked down the hall into his office
and you killed him?

711
00:42:47,040 --> 00:42:48,075
No.

712
00:42:49,240 --> 00:42:51,435
Listen to the chimes.

713
00:43:06,720 --> 00:43:11,748
The chimes in this clock
match the chimes on the tape.

714
00:43:14,080 --> 00:43:16,833
You did place that call
from your office.

715
00:43:17,360 --> 00:43:21,114
You did kill Alan Rollins
for having an affair with your wife.

716
00:43:24,000 --> 00:43:26,070
But you killed the wrong man.

717
00:43:33,600 --> 00:43:35,318
Your Honour,

718
00:43:35,480 --> 00:43:37,914
in light of the evidence
and in the interest of justice,

719
00:43:38,080 --> 00:43:39,354
the People move to dismiss.

720
00:43:40,600 --> 00:43:45,071
Motion granted. Case dismissed.
Court is adjourned.

721
00:43:48,080 --> 00:43:49,559
- I owe you.
- I figure it's them.

722
00:43:49,720 --> 00:43:51,631
They're your friends,
whether you like it or not.

723
00:43:51,800 --> 00:43:52,949
Thank you.

724
00:43:53,120 --> 00:43:55,031
I have a Christmas present
for all of you as well.

725
00:43:55,200 --> 00:43:57,191
Your building won't be torn down
after all.

726
00:43:58,800 --> 00:44:00,392
Well, I have a plane to catch.

727
00:44:00,560 --> 00:44:02,073
- Merry Christmas.
- And to you, Daddy.

728
00:44:02,240 --> 00:44:03,309
- Merry Christmas.
- Me too.

729
00:44:03,480 --> 00:44:05,948
Charlene, shall we dance?

730
00:44:42,920 --> 00:44:45,559
Merry Christmas.

731
00:44:45,720 --> 00:44:47,438
- I knew you were--
- No, you didn't.

732
00:44:47,600 --> 00:44:50,068
- Yes, I did.
- Merry Christmas, Ben.

733
00:44:50,240 --> 00:44:51,673
- Merry Christmas.
- Merry Christmas.

734
00:44:51,840 --> 00:44:53,193
Merry Christmas.

735
00:44:53,360 --> 00:44:57,194
You're the best Christmas present
